 We argue that reusable verification state should retain preparation while separating each candidate's execution history and lifetime. Preparing context before candidate evaluation makes that native state available to independent attempts. Each candidate inherits the prepared state, so preceding alternatives neither supply its solver history nor extend its lifetime. The benefit depends on preparation cost, branch creation cost and the pages that each candidate modifies.
 
